net_node: size the tree-string buffer so large trees don't overflow the heap

get_network_tree_string() malloc'd a fixed EM_LONG_IO_BUFF_SZ (16KB) buffer and
get_network_tree_node_string() appends into it with several mis-bounded
strncat()s (bounded by the source length, or by sizeof(char*)=4, rather than the
remaining buffer). A tree larger than 16KB therefore overflowed the heap and
aborted the next malloc/free with "corrupted top size" / "double free". A
3-device get_sta tree exceeds 16KB, which is what aborted steer_sta before it
could send (it worked on smaller meshes). Size the buffer to the subdoc buffer
the string is ultimately copied into (EM_MAX_EVENT_DATA_LEN).

diff --git a/src/em/em_net_node.cpp b/src/em/em_net_node.cpp
index eb3c29c8..a00de540 100644
--- a/src/em/em_net_node.cpp
+++ b/src/em/em_net_node.cpp
@@ -351,7 +351,13 @@ void em_net_node_t::get_network_tree_node_string(char *str, em_network_node_t *n
 char *em_net_node_t::get_network_tree_string(em_network_node_t *node)
 {
     unsigned int ident = 0;
-    unsigned int size = EM_LONG_IO_BUFF_SZ;
+    /* get_network_tree_node_string() serializes the whole tree into this buffer
+       with several mis-bounded strncat()s (bounded by the source length or by
+       sizeof(char*)=4), so a tree larger than the buffer overflows the heap and
+       aborts the next allocation/free. EM_LONG_IO_BUFF_SZ (16KB) is exceeded by a
+       multi-device get_sta tree, which is what crashed steer_sta before it could
+       send. Size the buffer to the subdoc buffer this string is copied into. */
+    unsigned int size = EM_MAX_EVENT_DATA_LEN;
     char *str;
 
     str = static_cast<char *> (malloc(size));
